In this conversation, Chip Huyen outlines several key arguments about AI evaluation:

Two distinct types of evals exist. The first is evaluating a specific AI application (like a chatbot) to determine whether it's performing well. The second is task-specific eval design for model developers, which involves deeply understanding a domain — like creative writing — and designing datasets and criteria to measure quality in that domain. Huyen emphasizes that this second type is surprisingly creative and fun work.

The "vibes vs. evals" debate has a rational business logic behind it. Some successful companies skip formal evals and rely on intuition — checking whether things feel like they're working. Huyen explains why this happens through the lens of an executive weighing trade-offs: if an app is already working well enough (customers are happy, traffic is growing), investing two engineers in evals for a marginal improvement from 80% to 85% may be less attractive than deploying those engineers to build an entirely new feature and get it to "good enough" as well.

But skipping evals carries real risk. Without clear metrics and visibility into how a model is performing, applications can fail in unexpected or damaging ways. Huyen argues that evals become critically important in three scenarios: when you operate at scale, when failures could have catastrophic consequences, and when product quality is a competitive differentiator — where you need to understand precisely where you stand relative to competitors.

The overall takeaway is that evals aren't universally necessary at the same depth for every company, but the decision to skip them is a calculated bet that becomes increasingly dangerous as stakes and scale grow.